ng groups using hypergraphs. ... To a set $\\B$ of 4-subsets of a set $\\Omega$ of size $n$ we introduce an invariant called the `hole stabilizer' which generalises a construction of Conway, Elkies and Martin of the Mathieu group $M_{12}$ based on Loyd's `15-puzzle'.\n\n# Generating groups using hypergraphs\n\nGill, Nick and Gillespie, Neil and Nixon, Anthony Keith and Semeraro, Jason (2016) Generating groups using hypergraphs. The Quarterly Journal of Mathematics, 67 (1). pp. 29-52. ISSN 0033-5606\n\nImage\n\nPreview\n\nPDF (gen)\ngen.pdf - Accepted Version\nAvailable under License Creative Commons Attribution.\nDownload (425kB)\n\n## Abstract\n\nTo a set $\\B$ of 4-subsets of a set $\\Omega$ of size $n$ we introduce an invariant called the `hole stabilizer' which generalises a construction of Conway, Elkies and Martin of the Mathieu group $M_{12}$ based on Loyd's `15-puzzle'. It is shown that hole stabilizers may be regarded as objects inside an objective partial group (in the sense of Chermak). We classify pairs $(\\Omega,\\B)$ with a trivial hole stabilizer, and determine all hole stabilizers associated to $2$-$(n,4,\\lambda)$ designs with $\\lambda \\leq 2$.\n\nItem Type:\n\nJournal Article\n\nJournal or Publication Title:\n\nThe Quarterly Journal of Mathematics\n\nAdditional Information:\n\nThis is a pre-copy-editing, author-produced PDF of an article accepted for publication in The Quarterly Journal of Mathematics following peer review. The definitive publisher-authenticated version Nick Gill, Neil I. Gillespie, Anthony Nixon, and Jason Semeraro GENERATING GROUPS USING HYPERGRAPHS Q J Math (2016) 67 (1): 29-52 doi:10.1093/qmath/haw001 is available online at: http://qjmath.oxfordjournals.org/content/67/1/29.abstract\n\nUncontrolled Keywords:\n\n/dk/atira/pure/subjectarea/asjc/2600/2600\n\nSubjects:\n\n?? general mathematicsmathematics(all) ??\n\nDepartments:\n\nFaculty of Science and Technology > Mathematics and Statistics\n\nID Code:\n\n78569\n\nDeposited By:\n\nep_importer_pure\n\nDeposited On:\n\n08 Mar 2016 16:00\n\nRefereed?:\n\nYes\n\nPublished?:\n\nPublished\n\nLast Modified:\n\n11 Dec 2025 01:15\n\nURI:\n\nhttps://eprints.lancs.ac.uk/id/eprint/78569--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics of Tableau Inversions (https://arxiv.org/abs/1412.6473)\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.9 years ago; Title: Combinatorics of Tableau Inversions ... A tableau inversion is a pair of entries in row-standard tableau $T$ that lie in the same column of $T$ yet lack the appropriate relative ordering to make $T$ column-standard. ... We develop formulas enumerating the number of $i$-inverted Young tableaux for a variety of tableaux shapes, not restricting ourselves to inverted tableaux that standardize a specific standard Young tableau, and construct bijections between $i$-inverted Young tableaux of a certain shape with $j$-inverted Young tableaux of different shapes.\nTitle: Combinatorics of Tableau Inversions\nAuthors: Jonathan E. Beagley, Paul Drube\nDate: Fri Dec 19 18:30:14 2014\n\nA tableau inversion is a pair of entries in row-standard tableau $T$ that lie in the same column of $T$ yet lack the appropriate relative ordering to make $T$ column-standard. An $i$-inverted Young tableau is a row-standard tableau along with a precisely $i$ inversion pairs. Tableau inversions were originally introduced by Fresse to calculate the Betti numbers of Springer fibers in Type A, with the number of $i$-inverted tableaux that standardize to a fixed standard Young tableau corresponding to a specific Betti number of the associated fiber. In this paper we approach the topic of tableau inversions from a completely combinatorial perspective. We develop formulas enumerating the number of $i$-inverted Young tableaux for a variety of tableaux shapes, not restricting ourselves to inverted tableaux that standardize a specific standard Young tableau, and construct bijections between $i$-inverted Young tableaux of a certain shape with $j$-inverted Young tableaux of different shapes. Finally, we share some the results of a computer program developed to calculate tableaux inversions.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInteger colorings with no rainbow k-term arithmetic progression - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669822000439)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Szemerédi’s Theorem [3] ... The anti-Ramsey (more precisely, anti-van der Waerden) properties of arithmetic progressions have received great attention as well (see [4], [5] and references therein). ... In [4], Butler et al. proved that for sufficiently large n, aw(\\left[n\\right],3)=\\Theta (logn) and aw(\\left[n\\right],k)={n}^{1-o\\left(1\\right)} for k\\ge 4.\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\n## Szemerédi’s Theorem [3]\n\nFor a positive integer k and a real number \\alpha >0, there exists a positive integer sz(k,\\alpha ) such that for all n\\ge sz(k,\\alpha ), every subset S\\subseteq \\left[n\\right] with \\left|S\\right|\\ge \\alpha n contains a k-AP.\n\nThe anti-Ramsey (more precisely, anti-van der Waerden) properties of arithmetic progressions have received great attention as well (see [4], [5] and references therein). Given a positive integer k and a set S, the anti-van der Waerden number aw(S,k) is the smallest r such that every exact r-coloring of S contains a rainbow k-AP. In [4], Butler et al. proved that for sufficiently large n, aw(\\left[n\\right],3)=\\Theta (logn) and aw(\\left[n\\right],k)={n}^{1-o\\left(1\\right)} for k\\ge 4. Note that this definition of aw(S,k) implies that there are always r-colorings of S with no rainbow k-AP for all r<aw(S,k). An r-coloring of S is called rainbow k-AP-free if it contains no rainbow k-AP.\n\nFollowing a recent trend of working on problems about counting certain colorings and analyzing extremal structure of them, for positive integers r, k and set S\\subseteq \\left[n\\right], we are interested in the problem of counting the number, {g}_{r,k}\\left(S\\right), of rainbow k-AP-free r-colorings of S. In addition, we naturally want to know, whether the behavior of {g}_{r,k}\\left(\\left[n\\right]\\right) is similar to that of aw(\\left[n\\right],k), which is different for k=3 and for k\\ge 4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAnti-van der Waerden numbers of 3-term arithmetic progressions (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/1604.08819)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Anti-van der Waerden numbers of 3-term arithmetic progressions ... Butler et. al. showed that $$\\lceil \\log_3 n \\rceil + 2 \\le aw([n],3) \\le \\lceil \\log_2 n \\rceil + 1$$⌈lo g 3​n⌉+2≤a w([n],3)≤⌈lo g 2​n⌉+1, and conjectured that there exists a constant $C$C such that $aw([n],3) \\le \\lceil \\log_3 n \\rceil + C$a w([n],3)≤⌈lo g 3​n⌉+C.\n\n# Anti-van der Waerden numbers of 3-term arithmetic progressions\n\nPublished 29 Apr 2016 in math.CO | (1604.08819v1)\n\nAbstract: The \\emph{anti-van der Waerden number}, denoted by $aw([n],k)$a w([n],k), is the smallest $r$r such that every exact $r$r-coloring of $[n]$[n] contains a rainbow $k$k-term arithmetic progression. Butler et. al. showed that $$\\lceil \\log_3 n \\rceil + 2 \\le aw([n],3) \\le \\lceil \\log_2 n \\rceil + 1$$⌈lo g 3​n⌉+2≤a w([n],3)≤⌈lo g 2​n⌉+1, and conjectured that there exists a constant $C$C such that $aw([n],3) \\le \\lceil \\log_3 n \\rceil + C$a w([n],3)≤⌈lo g 3​n⌉+C. In this paper, we show this conjecture is true by determining $aw([n],3)$a w([n],3) for all $n$n. We prove that for $$7\\cdot 3<sup>{m-2}+1</sup> \\leq n \\leq 21 \\cdot 3<sup>{m-2}$$7⋅3<s u p>m−2+1</s u p>≤n≤21⋅3<s u p>m−2, [ aw([n],3)=\\left{\\begin{array}{ll} m+2, & \\mbox{if $n=3<sup>m$n=3<s u p>m}\\ m+3, & \\mbox{otherwise}. \\end{array}\\right.]\n\nAuthors (3)\n\n  